Zengine @ZENGINE_PACKAGE_VERSION@ -- Workshop, ready to run

An alpha. Interfaces change without deprecation cycles.

START
  Windows  Workshop.cmd opens Workshop in a window; Workshop-terminal.cmd runs it in the console.
  Linux    ./workshop.sh opens Workshop in a window; ./workshop-terminal.sh runs it in a terminal.
  Where no window can open (no display, no video driver), the window's launcher goes on in the
  terminal it was started from and says why once there.
  Nothing else needs installing, and this folder may stand anywhere: a newer package replaces it
  whole, and nothing of yours is kept in it.

YOUR PROJECT
  Workshop's project is a folder of your own, made at the first start and kept across packages,
  and both launchers share it:
    Windows  %USERPROFILE%\Zengine
    Linux    ~/Zengine
  Saved views, flows, setups, a project plan (workshop-plan.json, which then starts instead of the
  shipped one, in either launcher's window) and the apps you export (apps/<name>/) go there.

  Each start writes zengine-app.path in the project: the zengine-app of the package that started
  it. An exported app's run.cmd and run.sh start that one, so an app runs with the newest package
  you started. If that package is gone, the app says so: start Workshop once from the package you
  keep, and run the app again.

  Keymap and preferences:   Windows %APPDATA%\zengine-workshop      Linux ~/.config/zengine-workshop
  The desk you quit with:   Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%\zengine-workshop Linux ~/.local/state/zengine-workshop

FIRST STEPS
  Ctrl+Space lists every action and pane by name. The View Builder makes a view; Flow answers it;
  the View Builder's Export app writes it as an app, and Run app starts it in a window of its own
  that goes on after Workshop quits.

WHAT RUNS
  Workshop loads the files its plan in bin/ names, in its own process, trusted, with no sandbox.
  It opens no network port unless started with --guests. An app asks at its own console before
  each file of its plan runs. bin/zengine-workshop --version names this package's commits.

BUILDING A PANE
  This Workshop builds nothing yet: it names no CMake. This folder is also the prefix a pane
  builds against (include/, lib/, lib/cmake/); BUILD-INFO.txt names the compiler it was built with.

SOURCE AND LICENSES
  LICENSE (MPL-2.0) covers Zengine and the Loom; NOTICE.md lists everything else in this folder
  and where its license text is. The source is at https://github.com/Krealsion/Zengine and
  https://github.com/Krealsion/Loom, at the commits BUILD-INFO.txt names.
